You are checking whether a mathematical expression will be read aloud correctly. You are given the expression in LaTeX and the plain-text form prepared for a text-to-speech voice. Answer one question per entry: would a listener hearing the plain text understand the same mathematics the LaTeX states?

{{ judge_instructions }}

Judging rules:
- Judge only whether the mathematics survives. Do NOT ask for a more conventional or prettier rendering.
- Symbols are expected and correct. `2/5`, `×`, `÷`, `π`, `mm²` are all acceptable — the voice localises them. Never mark an entry down for failing to spell numbers out as words.
- Mark `acceptable: false` when a number, variable or operator differs from the LaTeX; when the grouping differs so the value would be heard differently; when notation with no spoken form survives (a caret, an underscore, a stray accent, an unmatched bracket); or when content in the LaTeX is missing from the text.
- A wrong value is the most serious finding — it sounds authoritative while stating the wrong mathematics. Use `severity: high` for it.
{% if generate_suggestions %}- When an entry is unacceptable, return `suggested_text`: the same mathematics written the way it should be spoken, in the language of the surrounding book. Return it ONLY when you are certain it states the mathematics exactly — a wrong suggestion is worse than none.
{% else %}- Do NOT return `suggested_text`.
{% endif %}{% if additional_guidance %}
